{"id":137,"date":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/seven-warning-signs-an-ephrata-furnace-gives-before-it-quits\/"},"modified":"2026-07-14T19:43:57","modified_gmt":"2026-07-14T19:43:57","slug":"seven-warning-signs-an-ephrata-furnace-gives-before-it-quits","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/seven-warning-signs-an-ephrata-furnace-gives-before-it-quits\/","title":{"rendered":"Three Building Eras, Three HVAC Problems \u2014 Ephrata by the Block"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text alignfull has-media-on-the-right s-hero-split wp-elements-826a1d42de01731e1172a5b59e22d034\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading s-hero-split__text wp-block-heading\">Three centuries demand different solutions<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Your neighborhood illustrates Pennsylvania residential history in layers: Cloister-era stone buildings on one block, 1960s ranches on the next, and modern construction filling the gaps\u2014each era needs a different HVAC approach.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Schedule the Walkthrough<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-rowhouse-1.png\" alt=\"rowhouse - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1769\" style=\"object-fit:cover;height:100%\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-rowhouse-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-rowhouse-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-rowhouse-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-rowhouse-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Architectural diversity requires architectural thinking<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Few communities display such compressed architectural diversity. The Ephrata Cloister historic district preserves Germanic medieval-inspired buildings from the 1740s constructed with massive timber framing and stone. One street over, post-war ranches from the 1960s suburban expansion sit on slab foundations with minimal insulation and original single-pane windows. Scattered among them, infill construction from the 2000s brings modern building codes but often builder-grade execution.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This diversity means there is no standard Ephrata HVAC solution. What works brilliantly in a century home fails utterly in a mid-century ranch, and new construction presents entirely different challenges. Homeowners and contractors who apply one-size-fits-all thinking miss the mark entirely. The proper system for your home depends on which era it represents and what modifications have occurred since original construction.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Historic high-mass buildings need slow steady heat<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Buildings from Ephrata&#039;s founding period through the mid-1800s share common characteristics that define their HVAC requirements. Thick masonry or log walls provide substantial thermal mass. Small, strategically placed windows minimize heat loss. High ceilings and compact floor plans reflect pre-modern comfort expectations. These buildings were designed to be heated by wood or coal stoves, and many still have original chimney systems.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The appropriate heating approach prioritizes slow, steady heat delivery over rapid response. Hydronic systems using radiators or radiant floors match the building&#039;s thermal time constant perfectly. A modern condensing boiler provides efficient heat generation, and distribution through radiators or in-floor tubing delivers warmth gradually, allowing the building mass to absorb and store heat. This eliminates the cold-wall discomfort common when heating high-mass buildings with forced air systems.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If forced-air heating is chosen, it must deliver consistent low-level heat rather than short, high-output cycles. Two-stage furnaces or modulating systems that run extended cycles at partial capacity provide better comfort than single-stage equipment. Thermostat setback should be minimal or eliminated entirely\u2014high-mass buildings penalize aggressive setback with long recovery times and excessive energy consumption.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-2.png\" alt=\"furnace - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1758\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-furnace-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mid-century ranches: compensating for envelope weakness<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ranch homes built throughout Ephrata&#039;s residential expansion between 1955 and 1975 represent a distinct challenge set. These homes typically feature slab-on-grade foundations, minimal insulation by modern standards, extensive single-pane window area, and open floor plans. The HVAC systems originally installed were sized generously to compensate for envelope weakness, and many now fail or operate grossly inefficiently.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Addressing comfort in mid-century ranches begins with acknowledging that the building envelope is the primary problem. Upgrading insulation to modern standards proves difficult\u2014walls are typically 2&#215;4 construction with fiberglass batts yielding R-11 to R-13. Attics are more accessible: bringing attic insulation to R-49 or R-60 with blown cellulose reduces heat loss substantially.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Air sealing delivers high returns\u2014weatherstripping doors, caulking window frames, sealing the band joist where foundation meets framing\u2014reducing infiltration that accounts for 25-40% of heating load in these leaky buildings. Window replacement or storm window addition addresses another major loss pathway. The picture windows and sliding glass doors popular in 1960s design create massive heat loss with single-pane glass.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Replacement with double-pane low-E glass improves the window U-factor from 1.1 to 0.30 or better, cutting window heat loss by nearly 75%. Once envelope improvements are in place, HVAC equipment can be properly sized for the reduced load rather than compensating for an inefficient building. The combination of envelope work and right-sized equipment often cuts heating costs by 40-50% compared to simply replacing an oversized furnace with another oversized furnace.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">New construction: diagnosing builder-grade shortfalls<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ephrata&#039;s residential growth continues with infill development and subdivision expansion, bringing homes built under modern energy codes with tighter envelopes and more efficient windows than mid-century construction. These homes should perform well, and yet complaints about comfort, high bills, and premature equipment failure are common. The issue is rarely the equipment specification but rather installation quality and duct system design that falls short.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New construction HVAC installations in volume-builder homes are typically bid at fixed prices with minimal engineering. Equipment is sized using software based on square footage and generic assumptions rather than room-by-room load calculations. Ductwork is designed by the installation crew in the field, sized by rules of thumb, and installed to fit around framing rather than following calculated design.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Refrigerant charge is set using ambient temperature charts rather than precise superheat measurements. Startup consists of verifying the system runs; airflow verification, duct leakage testing, and combustion analysis are skipped to save time. These shortcuts create systems that function but underperform. Common symptoms include temperature imbalance between rooms, short-cycling equipment, high utility bills relative to home size, and early compressor or heat exchanger failures.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Diagnosis requires systematic testing: measuring airflow at registers, testing duct leakage with a blower door, verifying refrigerant charge with manifold gauges, and evaluating thermostat location. Most new-construction comfort problems trace to correctable installation deficiencies rather than equipment defects.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1755\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">System selection by building era<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Given Ephrata&#039;s architectural diversity, system selection must be tailored to the building type rather than following generic recommendations. Each era&#039;s construction characteristics suggest different optimal approaches.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Historic high-mass buildings: Hydronic heating with radiators or radiant floors. Modulating equipment if forced-air. Mini-splits for cooling without ductwork intrusion.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Mid-century ranches with weak envelopes: Prioritize envelope improvements first. Then right-size equipment to improved load. Multi-stage systems for better comfort than single-stage.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>1980s-2000s two-story homes: Two-zone systems with separate thermostats for living and sleeping areas. Address upper-floor overheating with transfer grilles or additional returns.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Recent new construction: Focus on diagnostics and corrections rather than replacement. Test and correct refrigerant charge, airflow, and duct leakage. Add zoning if needed.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Mixed-era homes with additions: Treat each section according to its construction type. Separate heating systems often work better than unified equipment for diverse eras.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When to repair versus replace<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ephrata homeowners face equipment decisions complicated by the diversity of building types and system ages. A 30-year-old furnace in a mid-century ranch may be wildly oversized for the current building after envelope improvements, making replacement with properly sized equipment cost-effective. The same-aged furnace in a historic high-mass building might be perfectly sized and worth repairing if mechanically sound. Decision criteria must account for building context, not just equipment age.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Repair makes sense when the existing equipment is appropriately sized and typed for the building, the failure is a component rather than systemic, and the overall system design is sound. A failed blower motor or cracked heat exchanger in a correctly sized furnace with well-designed ductwork justifies repair even if the equipment is 20 years old.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Conversely, replacing a functional but grossly oversized furnace makes sense when envelope improvements have reduced load substantially, because the oversized unit will short-cycle and provide poor comfort regardless of mechanical condition. Replacement becomes necessary when equipment is undersized for load, uses obsolete refrigerants, has efficiency far below current standards, or when the duct system is so poorly designed that the equipment cannot deliver comfort regardless of condition.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Many Ephrata homes with original or early equipment fall into this category\u2014a 60% AFUE furnace from 1985 uses 40% more energy than a 95% AFUE unit, paying for itself in energy savings over 10-15 years. The decision framework should weigh building characteristics, existing system appropriateness, repair costs relative to replacement, and energy savings potential.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png\" alt=\"condenser - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1767\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--outline\">\n<li>Building stock spans 1740s stone to 2010s frame<\/li>\n\n\n<li>No single HVAC approach fits all eras<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Northern Lancaster County: 5% higher heating loads<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Enhanced filtration (MERV-13) extends equipment life<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Historic properties: equipment concealment required<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Stone foundations: wick moisture into basements<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mixed-era building assessment<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Original section and addition have different comfort levels \u2014 separate load zones needed<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Stone walls meet frame walls \u2014 thermal bridging at the connection<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Equipment installed for original footprint struggles with addition \u2014 undersized for total volume<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Dust accumulates faster near ag operations \u2014 enhanced filtration extends equipment life<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Historic section has no duct access \u2014 ductless supplement needed for those rooms<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Ephrata HVAC across building eras<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Should I use the same HVAC approach in my 1740s stone house as my neighbor uses in their 1968 ranch?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Absolutely not. High-mass historic construction requires heating strategies that accommodate slow thermal response and minimal setback. Mid-century ranches with weak envelopes need envelope improvements first and then right-sized equipment. Applying a ranch-appropriate system to a historic stone building will create comfort problems and waste energy. The building type dictates the appropriate HVAC approach more than geography or climate. Work with contractors who understand these differences and design systems specific to your building&#039;s era and construction.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>My 1960s home has the original furnace. Should I replace it even though it still runs?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Furnaces from the 1960s typically operate at 60-65% AFUE, meaning 35-40% of the fuel you burn goes up the chimney. Modern condensing furnaces achieve 95-96% AFUE, reducing fuel consumption by one-third for the same heat output. If the existing furnace is oversized\u2014common in that era\u2014replacement with properly sized high-efficiency equipment improves comfort while cutting costs. However, address envelope weaknesses first through air sealing and insulation upgrades;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">otherwise, the new furnace will be oversized as well. The combination of envelope work and right-sized modern equipment typically pays for itself in 8-12 years through fuel savings alone.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I cool my historic home with central air without destroying its character?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, with thoughtful planning. Ductless mini-split systems avoid the ductwork installation challenges that compromise historic interiors. Units can be positioned discreetly, and refrigerant lines require only small penetrations easily concealed. If ducted central air is preferred, routing ducts through unfinished attics and basements, using closet chases, or installing high-velocity systems with small flexible ducts minimizes intrusion.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Work with contractors experienced in historic retrofits who understand the need to preserve character-defining features while meeting comfort goals. Many historic Ephrata homes now have effective cooling systems installed with minimal visual or structural impact.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-nearby has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Also serving nearby:<\/strong> <a href=\"\/living-above-the-shop-on-main-street-lititz-apartment-comfort\/\">Lititz<\/a> \u00b7 <a href=\"\/older-home-modern-comfort-heating-care-for-lancaster-rowhouses\/\">Lancaster<\/a><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-1 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" data-id=\"1756\" src=\"\/w